From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:27:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Optionally keep short compute
 commands on one queue

With g16_compute_queues=2 every independent compute command goes to the
firmware queue after the previous one's. That overlaps long commands,
but short commands gain nothing from a second queue, and rotating them
made light compute about 1 us per command slower on experimental 1.1075
(20.1 against 19.1 us at depth 64) while heavy compute got 8.9 us
faster.

Add g16_cq_split_us (runtime, default 0). With N above 0 and more than
one compute queue, the kernel keeps for each client (VM) a moving
average of the GPU span of its retired compute commands, read from the
start and end stamps the firmware writes into the command's unit, each
new command weighing 1/4. While that average is under N us, or before
any command of the client has retired, a command the placement would
rotate stays on the queue of the client's previous compute command.
Longer clients rotate as before. A client that mixes both is judged on
its average.

Registration of an unregistered queue on an idle engine, the wait for
running work a command names, and the queue-order modes are unchanged.
A VM slot taken over by a new VM starts with no span. The statistics
gain a cq_split line with the word and the placements it changed.

With g16_cq_split_us=0, the default, or one compute queue, placement is
unchanged.

Assisted-by: LLM
